 m with 28\u00b0, and 4.5 at 40 m with 48\u00b0. The shear exponents come out to roughly 0.35 and 0.12, though the 150 m value looks noisy. I'm also estimating the veer from the 40 m and 100 m levels, which suggests something near -27\u00b0.\n\nI'm checking the veer estimate again: the 150 m level gives about -8\u00b0, while the 40 m level gives -27\u00b0, so the noise is large enough that I'll settle on around -15\u00b0. Next I'm moving on to the updraft and drag questions, starting with a drop through the smoke at low altitude."}, {"before_call": 6, "text": "The fall time came out longer than the measured one by 0.19 s at 100 m, which means the drag is too high rather than too low, since higher drag shortens the fall. So I'll lower the drag coefficient and try 0.025."}, {"before_call": 7, "text": "I'm settling the drag at roughly 0.0275, and next I'm testing drop positions: one low through the smoke's center, one starting at (-40,0) with heading 90, speed 45, and altitude 60. I want to see how far the load travels horizontally, probably around 100 m, and I'll run a simulation with an updraft guess of 5 to pick the best release."}, {"before_call": 8, "text": "I'm checking that the impacts land near the center when there's no updraft, and that an updraft makes the fall take longer, so the two cases come out different. Next I'll run the test drop to confirm."}, {"before_call": 9, "text": "The fall time came in at 6.47 against a predicted 5.98, so the gap is real but the updraft looks fairly small. I'll compare it against the second updraft case."}, {"before_call": 10, "text": "I'm trying a larger updraft value of around 6, since the current estimate falls short of the needed correction and the drag effects add uncertainty."}, {"before_call": 11, "text": "Fall times check out, but the y residuals run consistently positive, so the predicted y is too high. The x residuals lean the same direction, which points to the wind needing to shift a bit further east. I'll try a wind direction of 38.\n\nI'm also planning more test drops at different altitudes and speeds, since the targets differ in position and intensity. Then I'll try the wind direction at 38."}, {"before_call": 12, "text": "Test 2's dx came out at 5.1, so only dy is still unresolved. I want more data under varied conditions, so I'm planning six more test drops. Drop 5 is high speed (80) at 150 m heading 270, landing far out at (400, 100). Drop 6 is a low 30 m altitude at speed 60 heading 90, starting from (-200, 0), which may pass through the smoke, so I'll simulate it next.\n\nI'm going to skip simulating the low pass through the smoke and just run the tests. Targets sit far from the calibration fire, so the wind should be the same there, and tests away from the smoke should give cleaner wind information. I'll do a few of them."}, {"before_call": 15, "text": "I'm reading the wind measurements at each height, and they seem to shift direction as altitude increases. Taking the direction readings from 60 m up to 150 m, they fall from about 50 to 27.5 degrees, which works out to roughly a -25 degree veer per 100 m.\n\nThe 30 m and 40 m readings run flatter, about 47 to 48 degrees, so they sit off the trend, probably from noise of around 4 degrees. Taking the direction at 100 m as roughly 35 degrees gives a veer near -22. For speed, the 30 m value of 4.1 against the 6.2 near the top suggests a shear exponent of about 0.34, while the upper levels are close to flat, so I'll settle on a wind speed of 6.0 and a shear of 0.25 and compare that against the data."}, {"before_call": 16, "text": "Positive time differences in tests 4, 5, and 6 mean the predicted fall times are running long, so I'll try a slightly lower drag or a weaker updraft. Next I'm testing test 4 with dy at 3.5, along with drag at 0.026 and updraft at 5."}, {"before_call": 17, "text": "I'm leaning toward a drag of 0.027 and an updraft of 5.5 as a reasonable fit, though I'm not fully convinced. With three test drops left, I'm considering running them through the smoke at different positions to pin down the updraft, especially at heavy intensity, since the targets range from 0.5 to 1.4.\n\nI'm settling on a plan to place two drops through the smoke, one at 40 m heading 90 with release near (-35,-5), and one at 150 m, where the updraft acts over a longer stretch of the fall. For the higher drop I want to run a simulation to find a usable release point."}, {"before_call": 19, "text": "The fall time came out to 11.69 for the 150 case, compared with 9.73 without, so the sensitivity looks good. I'll go ahead with the 150 run."}, {"before_call": 21, "text": "The result looks fairly good overall. Test 8's negative dt points toward a bit more updraft, maybe around 6.5, though test 4's positive dt suggests the opposite, so the profile differences need a closer look. I'll try updraft at 6.5 and see how it behaves."}, {"before_call": 22, "text": "I'm seeing conflicting signals here: test 8's positive dx points to too far east, while test 4's dt suggests the prediction runs too long. I'm thinking the updraft load might explain the dx result, so I'll try small tweaks to the mid-altitude wind, setting updraft to 6.2 and wind speed to 5.8, and also changing shear to 0.3."}, {"before_call": 23, "text": "The residuals look acceptable at this noise level, so I'm weighing whether to push the wind direction to 36 degrees, since the y-residuals in runs 4, 6, 3 and 7 lean positive. With two test drops left, I'm considering using them to check a different regime, but test drops are no longer possible once operation begins, so they'd have to serve as validation near the edge of the smoke, perhaps with the updraft off-axis.\n\nI'm considering one more test drop at altitude 100, speed 60, heading 200, passing through the smoke off-center, to compare against the predictions before I commit to the remaining plan."}, {"before_call": 24, "text": "I'm ruling out smoke as the cause, so I'll try a heading of 200 from (60,200) and just run a test: x=20, y=130, altitude 100, speed 60, heading 200. That should take it roughly 70 meters south, and I'll simulate it once to check."}, {"before_call": 25, "text": "The fall time of 7.12 doesn't show up in the smoke even though the figures are close. Load stops at y=83 outside, so I'm going with y=80."}, {"before_call": 27, "text": "The wind readings at 100m came in at 5.7 and 6.2 at different heights, and the direction figure there looks like noise, so I'll set it aside. The hypothesis still holds. I'll leave the last test drop unused and move on to starting the operation."}, {"before_call": 28, "text": "I'm working through each target by simulating from a rough starting release point and then shifting that point by the miss, repeating until it converges. The smoke's updraft makes the motion nonlinear, so I expect to need a few passes, and I'm treating the updraft strength as scaling with intensity. For the first target I'm estimating the fall time and displacement from its heading and speed, then picking an initial release offset from the target.\n\nI'm placing the first release point about 100 units from the target along the heading, which puts it near (497, 981)."}, {"before_call": 33, "text": "I'm correcting the release point by the negative of the miss, which gives about (466.7, 972.3). The smoke changes the path, though, so the first correction may not hold and I'll need to iterate on the second throw.\n\nI'm working through the remaining throws, computing each release point as the target's error subtracted from the previous release: the second one lands near (1183, 122.4), the third near (1165.9, -962.2), the fourth near (-1886.6, 117.1), and the fifth near (-1018.2, 15.4)."}, {"before_call": 38, "text": "I'm checking each trial's error and shifting the release point to correct it, with the fourth trial already fine. The smoke effect is nonlinear and sensitive to model errors, since the updraft changes the fall time a lot, so I'm worried about robustness.\n\nI'm accepting that the on-axis updraft makes the result sensitive to errors in its parameters, and that there isn't much I can do about it. I'll just run one more iteration on trials 3 and 5."}, {"before_call": 40, "text": "I'm weighing whether any of the choices would make the result less sensitive, though that's hard to pin down. A small sensitivity check on the hypothesis doesn't seem worth the effort, so I'm dropping it and moving on."}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 318301, "completion_tokens": 12772, "cache_read_tokens": 299915, "requests": 27}, "stopped": "done"}, "agent": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "run": 3}